1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
History's original heavy metal,

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:06,000
unearthed from a dark underworld

3
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
and processed by an arsenal of machinery.

4
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:13,000
18 pounds will start your car,

5
00:00:13,001 --> 00:00:17,000
and a tiny particle will unlock secrets of the past.

6
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:20,000
It protects, but it also poisons,

7
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:23,000
and it's the key to making majestic music.

8
00:00:26,000 --> 00:00:29,000
Now, the untold story of lead,

9
00:00:29,001 --> 00:00:31,200
of lead on Modern Marvels.

10
00:00:47,220 --> 00:00:50,780
There's a good reason the world produces nearly 4 million tons

11
00:00:50,781 --> 00:00:51,860
of lead every year.

12
00:00:53,560 --> 00:00:56,800
We find it just as useful as our ancient ancestors did,

13
00:00:56,801 --> 00:00:59,300
who revered it as the father of all metals.

14
00:01:02,700 --> 00:01:05,280
Today, over 600 million drivers around the world

15
00:01:05,380 --> 00:01:07,920
depend on it every time they turn their ignition keys.

16
00:01:11,680 --> 00:01:14,380
An astounding 80% of all the lead we use

17
00:01:14,480 --> 00:01:17,880
ends up in lead-acid batteries.

18
00:01:17,980 --> 00:01:20,260
The battery under your hood consists of cells

19
00:01:20,360 --> 00:01:22,460
containing lead plates.

20
00:01:22,560 --> 00:01:24,800
Each plate is comprised of a lead grid

21
00:01:24,801 --> 00:01:27,940
and a lead oxide paste coating it.

22
00:01:28,040 --> 00:01:30,300
The plates, half of them positively charged,

23
00:01:30,400 --> 00:01:32,340
the other half negatively charged,

24
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:35,140
sit in a bath of sulfuric acid.

25
00:01:35,240 --> 00:01:37,940
The acid causes a chemical reaction in the lead,

26
00:01:38,040 --> 00:01:40,640
creating electric current.

27
00:01:40,740 --> 00:01:43,780
To reduce manufacturing costs, most car battery makers

28
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:47,260
use recycled lead and lead alloys that contain hardeners like

29
00:01:47,360 --> 00:01:49,360
calcium for their grids.

30
00:01:49,460 --> 00:01:51,900
These substances contain impurities that make the lead

31
00:01:51,901 --> 00:01:55,200
corrode faster in its harsh acid environment, which eventually

32
00:01:55,300 --> 00:01:58,440
causes the battery to fail.

33
00:01:58,540 --> 00:02:01,140
Enersys Energy Products in Warrensburg, Missouri,

34
00:02:01,240 --> 00:02:04,140
makes a longer lasting, more reliable battery.

35
00:02:04,240 --> 00:02:08,480
And the secret ingredient is virtually pure lead.

36
00:02:08,580 --> 00:02:12,220
This is 99.99% pure lead.

37
00:02:12,320 --> 00:02:17,420
From here to the end of this row represents one truckload

38
00:02:17,520 --> 00:02:18,460
of lead.

39
00:02:18,461 --> 00:02:22,060
So this is 2,500 pounds right here.

40
00:02:22,160 --> 00:02:25,200
You don't get very much on a truckload.

41
00:02:25,300 --> 00:02:27,200
Eners' pure lead batteries are designed

42
00:02:27,300 --> 00:02:30,200
to provide the ultimate in dependability,

43
00:02:30,300 --> 00:02:32,480
the kind hospitals and communication networks

44
00:02:32,580 --> 00:02:34,640
use during power outages.

45
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:37,580
But the company also makes one you can put under your hood,

46
00:02:37,680 --> 00:02:40,880
called the Odyssey battery.

47
00:02:40,980 --> 00:02:46,120
The pure lead, however, is just one reason why it's a cut above.

48
00:02:46,121 --> 00:02:49,720
The lid plates made here are just over 1 millimeter thick,

49
00:02:49,820 --> 00:02:51,420
up to five times thinner than those

50
00:02:51,520 --> 00:02:54,520
in conventional car batteries.

51
00:02:54,620 --> 00:02:57,240
A thinner plate allows Enersys to fit more plates

52
00:02:57,340 --> 00:03:00,860
into each cell, and that can make a huge difference.

53
00:03:00,960 --> 00:03:03,680
More plates mean more surface area.

54
00:03:03,780 --> 00:03:06,680
More surface area on the plates means

55
00:03:06,780 --> 00:03:10,180
that it's able to deliver more energy faster,

56
00:03:10,280 --> 00:03:16,120
and that means more reliability in starting your vehicle.

57
00:03:16,121 --> 00:03:18,820
The plate-making process begins with one-ton blocks

58
00:03:18,920 --> 00:03:21,400
of lead called sows.

59
00:03:21,500 --> 00:03:24,860
These sows will be transferred into our melting pot

60
00:03:24,960 --> 00:03:28,740
at 500 degrees C, and then the melted lead

61
00:03:28,840 --> 00:03:30,840
will be used to form battery grid.

62
00:03:33,400 --> 00:03:35,800
Once the molten lead hits this rotating drum,

63
00:03:35,900 --> 00:03:38,480
which is slightly cooler than lead's relatively low melting

64
00:03:38,580 --> 00:03:41,880
point of just 621 degrees Fahrenheit,

65
00:03:41,980 --> 00:03:45,180
it quickly solidifies.

66
00:03:45,181 --> 00:03:48,560
reel after reel, each holding 1,700 feet of lead,

67
00:03:48,660 --> 00:03:53,820
wait in line for the punch that will form the grid.

68
00:03:53,920 --> 00:03:57,400
The material here is passing through a reciprocating die,

69
00:03:57,500 --> 00:04:01,300
which is punching holes at about 500 strokes a minute.

70
00:04:01,400 --> 00:04:06,060
It comes out the other end as a clean, plexed grid.

71
00:04:06,160 --> 00:04:08,540
If it didn't have holes, there'd be no room

72
00:04:08,640 --> 00:04:09,840
for the active material.

73
00:04:12,580 --> 00:04:15,160
The active material, the lead iron

74
00:04:15,180 --> 00:04:18,480
paste that will coat each grid is applied further down the

75
00:04:18,580 --> 00:04:19,880
production line.

76
00:04:19,980 --> 00:04:22,920
So paste is being dropped from the pasters, which

77
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,180
are above us.

78
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:26,920
They're coming through this tube system, and they're being

79
00:04:27,020 --> 00:04:31,060
applied to the grid so that you have a layer of grid with a layer

80
00:04:31,160 --> 00:04:32,940
of paste on top of it.

81
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:37,940
It then goes into the roller where a sheet of pasting

82
00:04:38,040 --> 00:04:39,440
paper is applied.

83
00:04:39,540 --> 00:04:42,500
The purpose of the pasting paper is to prevent the lead from

84
00:04:42,501 --> 00:04:46,500
becoming airborne later on as the plates start to dry

85
00:04:46,600 --> 00:04:50,500
and the lead oxide becomes a powder.

86
00:04:50,600 --> 00:04:54,040
Next, the plates are cut, weighed, and flattened.

87
00:04:54,140 --> 00:04:56,440
Then after curing for several hours,

88
00:04:56,540 --> 00:05:00,460
they're encapsulated into individual battery cells.

89
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:04,660
But still more lead is necessary to complete them.

90
00:05:04,760 --> 00:05:09,860
Robots dip each cell into a shallow pool of molten lead.

91
00:05:09,861 --> 00:05:12,160
There, tabs protruding from each plate

92
00:05:12,260 --> 00:05:14,040
fit into steel molds.

93
00:05:14,140 --> 00:05:17,940
Within 15 seconds, the molten lead solidifies.

94
00:05:18,040 --> 00:05:21,000
This lead connects each plate to the next,

95
00:05:21,100 --> 00:05:25,640
ensuring that electric current can pass between them.

96
00:05:25,740 --> 00:05:27,480
Finally, the cells are welded together

97
00:05:27,580 --> 00:05:29,840
into a single battery.

98
00:05:29,940 --> 00:05:33,480
Machines inject the sulfuric acid,

99
00:05:33,580 --> 00:05:36,880
and the battery receives its first charge.

100
00:05:36,980 --> 00:05:39,320
The higher reliability batteries

101
00:05:39,321 --> 00:05:42,560
provide, even though they do cost a little more,

102
00:05:42,660 --> 00:05:47,120
it does increase the reliability of your vehicle.

103
00:05:47,220 --> 00:05:50,460
Centuries before we were getting a charge out of lead,

104
00:05:50,560 --> 00:05:54,460
ancient civilizations recognized its value and versatility.

105
00:05:57,980 --> 00:06:00,580
From roofing material over the gardens of Babylon

106
00:06:00,680 --> 00:06:03,080
to facial cosmetics of Egyptian pharaohs,

107
00:06:03,180 --> 00:06:06,680
Chinese coins, to the type on Gutenberg's revolutionary

108
00:06:06,780 --> 00:06:09,320
printing press, lead has aged a year

109
00:06:09,321 --> 00:06:14,120
existence for nearly 6,000 years.

110
00:06:14,220 --> 00:06:18,220
But it was Roman engineers that first used lead on a grand scale.

111
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:21,060
They took advantage of one of lead's key properties,

112
00:06:21,160 --> 00:06:25,800
its malleability, by shaping it into an intricate network

113
00:06:25,900 --> 00:06:28,540
of pipes that delivered water to the imperial capital.

114
00:06:31,840 --> 00:06:34,480
The vast plumbing system inspired the Romans' Latin name

115
00:06:34,580 --> 00:06:39,320
for lead, plumbum, which is why lead's atomic symbol is PB.

116
00:06:39,321 --> 00:06:42,920
Lead had another property the Romans exploited

117
00:06:43,020 --> 00:06:45,960
to make drinking wine more pleasurable,

118
00:06:46,060 --> 00:06:47,960
despite the fact that many even then

119
00:06:48,060 --> 00:06:50,060
knew of lead's toxicity.

120
00:06:50,160 --> 00:06:51,740
I cringe when I think about this,

121
00:06:51,840 --> 00:06:53,940
is they used it to make wine sweeter.

122
00:06:54,040 --> 00:06:56,980
Romans would boil wine in lead vessels.

123
00:06:57,080 --> 00:07:00,440
The lead would form a compound called lead acetate,

124
00:07:00,540 --> 00:07:02,940
and that has a sweet flavor.

125
00:07:03,040 --> 00:07:06,120
Some historians believe that drinking lead-sweetened wine

126
00:07:06,121 --> 00:07:08,760
and water flowing through lead pipes

127
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:10,600
may have so debilitated the Romans

128
00:07:10,700 --> 00:07:13,660
that it contributed to the empire's fall.

129
00:07:13,760 --> 00:07:15,900
The reason why it was suspected

130
00:07:16,000 --> 00:07:18,340
that lead could have had an impact on the decline

131
00:07:18,440 --> 00:07:20,800
of the Roman Empire was that there was so much lead around

132
00:07:20,900 --> 00:07:23,600
at that time, but in fact, there was so much limestone

133
00:07:23,700 --> 00:07:26,540
in the water that the lead pipes would have been coated

134
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:27,980
with limestone inside.

135
00:07:28,080 --> 00:07:31,080
So the amount of lead therein wouldn't have been enough

136
00:07:31,180 --> 00:07:34,480
to expose them to so much lead that their reproductive systems

137
00:07:34,481 --> 00:07:37,320
would have failed completely, or they would have gone completely

138
00:07:37,420 --> 00:07:38,420
mad.

139
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:41,020
So there just wasn't enough lead to go around Rome

140
00:07:41,120 --> 00:07:43,920
to make the empire fall.

141
00:07:44,020 --> 00:07:47,020
While lead poisoning may not have brought down an empire,

142
00:07:47,120 --> 00:07:52,340
a potentially deadly neurotoxin poses no less a threat today.

143
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:55,740
If you ingest or inhale lead, most passes from your body

144
00:07:55,840 --> 00:07:57,540
within a few weeks.

145
00:07:57,640 --> 00:07:59,780
But your body mistakes some of it for calcium,

146
00:07:59,880 --> 00:08:04,480
a vital mineral nutrient, and stores it in your bones.

147
00:08:04,481 --> 00:08:06,720
Whenever the bones release calcium into the blood,

148
00:08:06,820 --> 00:08:10,180
responding to your body's needs, the lead joins it

149
00:08:10,280 --> 00:08:13,660
and can make its way to any of your organs.

150
00:08:13,760 --> 00:08:16,860
The most vulnerable of all is your brain.

151
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:18,920
When lead gets into your brain,

152
00:08:19,020 --> 00:08:22,740
it keeps calcium from doing its job helping your nerves

153
00:08:22,840 --> 00:08:24,240
function properly.

154
00:08:24,340 --> 00:08:26,440
That causes brain damage and damage

155
00:08:26,540 --> 00:08:29,640
to the rest of the nervous system as well.

156
00:08:29,740 --> 00:08:31,440
Although lead in high enough doses

157
00:08:31,540 --> 00:08:34,040
can do irreparable harm, it can also

158
00:08:34,041 --> 00:08:38,640
protect you from a dose of another kind.

159
00:08:38,740 --> 00:08:41,480
Light Tech Incorporated near Philadelphia, Pennsylvania,

160
00:08:41,580 --> 00:08:45,080
produces lead aprons, the kind you wear whenever you have an

161
00:08:45,180 --> 00:08:47,280
x-ray.

162
00:08:47,380 --> 00:08:50,420
Lead's high density and low cost have made it the ideal apron

163
00:08:50,520 --> 00:08:52,700
material for decades.

164
00:08:52,800 --> 00:08:55,400
There are many different styles of apron.

165
00:08:55,500 --> 00:08:57,100
This is perhaps the simplest one.

166
00:08:57,200 --> 00:08:59,240
This is a frontal protection only.

167
00:08:59,340 --> 00:09:02,800
This might have six, six and a half pounds of lead powder in it.

168
00:09:02,801 --> 00:09:06,400
When we get into higher exposures,

169
00:09:06,500 --> 00:09:10,640
either for time reasons or because the energy of the beam

170
00:09:10,740 --> 00:09:13,740
is much higher, typically in cardiology

171
00:09:13,840 --> 00:09:16,520
or cardiac catheterization, we're

172
00:09:16,620 --> 00:09:21,060
into a two-sided apron, front and back.

173
00:09:21,160 --> 00:09:25,760
This apron would have about 12 pounds of lead in it.

174
00:09:25,860 --> 00:09:30,900
Light-Tex aprons contain as much as 85% lead powder.

175
00:09:30,901 --> 00:09:33,560
The remainder consists of a variety of plastics that

176
00:09:33,660 --> 00:09:35,900
make the aprons flexible.

177
00:09:36,000 --> 00:09:39,800
The proprietary mixing process takes 20 minutes.

178
00:09:39,900 --> 00:09:44,640
This is a sheeting line which is converting the mixed material

179
00:09:44,740 --> 00:09:49,560
into a sheet, a very thin sheet, a very controlled sheet.

180
00:09:49,660 --> 00:09:55,360
The material is, this case, is about 15,000ths of an inch.

181
00:09:55,460 --> 00:09:58,860
That is to make it heavy enough, stop the radiation,

182
00:09:58,861 --> 00:10:02,160
but light enough so it's not too heavy for the doctor to wear.

183
00:10:02,260 --> 00:10:06,300
And one batch of 240 pounds from the mixer

184
00:10:06,400 --> 00:10:08,400
will make approximately two rolls.

185
00:10:08,500 --> 00:10:13,400
These rolls weigh about 120 pounds.

186
00:10:13,500 --> 00:10:16,440
Quality control supervisors cut out and weigh samples

187
00:10:16,540 --> 00:10:20,620
taken from the beginning and end of each roll.

188
00:10:20,720 --> 00:10:23,860
We don't want it to be too thin where it doesn't protect.

189
00:10:23,960 --> 00:10:28,360
We also don't want it to be too thick where it's heavy.

190
00:10:28,361 --> 00:10:31,360
In Light Tech's sewing room, workers stack the sheets up

191
00:10:31,460 --> 00:10:34,500
to four layers thick, depending on the level of protection

192
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:38,100
needed, and cut them to size.

193
00:10:38,200 --> 00:10:39,800
The lead is soft.

194
00:10:39,900 --> 00:10:41,900
It's a lot easier to just cut through like that.

195
00:10:42,000 --> 00:10:43,400
It slides right through.

196
00:10:43,500 --> 00:10:46,220
It's kind of like a knife through butter.

197
00:10:46,320 --> 00:10:49,220
Finally, they stitch the lead into vinyl coverings.

198
00:10:49,320 --> 00:10:53,420
And another lead apron is ready for your next x-ray exam.

199
00:10:53,520 --> 00:10:55,960
But what's precisely happening to those x-rays when they hit

200
00:10:56,060 --> 00:10:57,160
the lead?

201
00:10:57,161 --> 00:11:00,060
They can actually come in rather like a pinball machine.

202
00:11:00,160 --> 00:11:03,100
They can bounce off the lead atom, lose energy,

203
00:11:03,200 --> 00:11:06,740
and then pinball off another atom of lead,

204
00:11:06,840 --> 00:11:10,080
and reduce energy again until all the energy is dissipated.

205
00:11:10,180 --> 00:11:12,480
And there's no change to the lead.

206
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:16,140
Before lead can make its way into the x-ray room

207
00:11:16,240 --> 00:11:21,160
or under our hood, we've got to extract it from a dark world

208
00:11:21,260 --> 00:11:22,660
a thousand feet underground.

209
00:11:27,160 --> 00:11:30,200
Mining and smelting metals is a challenge measured not only

210
00:11:30,300 --> 00:11:33,040
by its technological complexity, but also

211
00:11:33,140 --> 00:11:34,500
by its environmental impact.

212
00:11:38,640 --> 00:11:43,640
This is especially true for a neurotoxin like lead.

213
00:11:43,740 --> 00:11:45,920
Only one primary lead smelting facility

214
00:11:46,020 --> 00:11:50,380
remains in the United States, in Herculaneum, Missouri,

215
00:11:50,480 --> 00:11:57,160
where a succession of companies has operated since 1892.

216
00:11:57,161 --> 00:12:00,800
In recent years, the smelter has become a focus of controversy

217
00:12:00,900 --> 00:12:03,900
as America tries to balance its dependence on lead

218
00:12:04,000 --> 00:12:06,500
against the environmental consequences of producing it.

219
00:12:09,500 --> 00:12:12,200
But the smelting operation is just one part of a complex

220
00:12:12,300 --> 00:12:16,620
process that begins at sites like this one, the Brushy Creek

221
00:12:16,720 --> 00:12:20,420
Mine outside the small town of Viburnum.

222
00:12:20,520 --> 00:12:23,960
Each morning, workers descend 1,000 feet below the Earth's

223
00:12:24,060 --> 00:12:27,160
surface to mine lead.

224
00:12:27,161 --> 00:12:29,800
The mine, operated by the Doe Run Company,

225
00:12:29,900 --> 00:12:33,240
is one of six lead mines far beneath southeastern Missouri's

226
00:12:33,340 --> 00:12:35,160
Ozark Mountain region.

227
00:12:35,260 --> 00:12:37,100
Over the past five decades, miners

228
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:39,600
have carved out this vast underworld,

229
00:12:39,700 --> 00:12:43,740
tapping one of the largest deposits of lead ore on Earth.

230
00:12:43,840 --> 00:12:48,180
They extract about 6 million tons of lead ore every year.

231
00:12:48,280 --> 00:12:50,780
Most people think that we mine straight lead,

232
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:53,820
and that's not necessarily the case.

233
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:56,420
We mine Galena.

234
00:12:56,421 --> 00:13:00,900
The main ore of lead is galena, lead sulfide.

235
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:06,000
It is made up of about 87% lead, and the rest is sulfur.

236
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:07,560
The formation of this deposit

237
00:13:07,660 --> 00:13:10,460
began more than 500 million years ago,

238
00:13:10,560 --> 00:13:13,200
when Missouri was under an ocean featuring a porous

239
00:13:13,300 --> 00:13:16,000
tropical reef.

240
00:13:16,100 --> 00:13:19,380
Over time, the ocean waters receded, the Earth's crust

241
00:13:19,480 --> 00:13:23,920
shifted, and mountains formed over the exposed reef.

242
00:13:23,921 --> 00:13:26,760
As warm groundwater migrated towards the mountains,

243
00:13:26,860 --> 00:13:29,420
it picked up minerals, including galena,

244
00:13:29,520 --> 00:13:32,660
and deposited them in the countless pores in the reef.

245
00:13:32,760 --> 00:13:34,600
And it looks just like this when it's

246
00:13:34,700 --> 00:13:38,400
in the ground in the host rock.

247
00:13:38,500 --> 00:13:42,280
The lead mining process begins as hydraulic drills bore blast

248
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:46,980
holes 14 feet deep into the ore face.

249
00:13:47,080 --> 00:13:49,580
A steady stream of water forces the loosened rock

250
00:13:49,680 --> 00:13:51,820
from the holes.

251
00:13:51,821 --> 00:13:56,820
After two hours, 65 precisely placed holes are complete.

252
00:13:56,920 --> 00:14:00,820
Most blast holes are about two inches in diameter.

253
00:14:00,920 --> 00:14:02,560
But within the center of the face,

254
00:14:02,660 --> 00:14:05,160
the drills bore larger holes to leave room

255
00:14:05,260 --> 00:14:07,500
for the exploding rock to expand.

256
00:14:07,600 --> 00:14:09,480
The rock has to have somewhere to break.

257
00:14:09,580 --> 00:14:12,940
It would kind of be like blowing up a balloon in a coffee can.

258
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:14,940
There's really nowhere for the rock to go.

259
00:14:15,040 --> 00:14:18,940
So we'll usually drill three larger size holes in the center,

260
00:14:19,040 --> 00:14:21,820
right dead center.

261
00:14:21,821 --> 00:14:24,460
The crew loads each hole with explosives containing

262
00:14:24,560 --> 00:14:27,320
the appropriate time-delay blasting cap.

263
00:14:27,420 --> 00:14:29,160
Blasting caps are numbered in accordance

264
00:14:29,260 --> 00:14:31,760
with their time delay.

265
00:14:31,860 --> 00:14:34,260
They increase outward from the center

266
00:14:34,360 --> 00:14:37,460
and detonate in a rapid-fire sequence.

267
00:14:37,560 --> 00:14:39,300
This will force the rock to cascade

268
00:14:39,400 --> 00:14:42,180
towards the center of the face.

269
00:14:42,280 --> 00:14:45,180
Once the area is clear of miners and machinery,

270
00:14:45,280 --> 00:14:47,580
the crew chief initiates his safety fuse

271
00:14:47,680 --> 00:14:51,820
and has two minutes to retreat to a safe distance.

272
00:14:51,821 --> 00:14:55,660
A half mile behind him, lead locked in place for 300 million

273
00:14:55,760 --> 00:14:57,760
years is about to be blasted free.

274
00:15:06,760 --> 00:15:09,300
When the dust settles, miners enter the blasted area

275
00:15:09,400 --> 00:15:12,900
and scrape any loose rock from the walls.

276
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:17,520
One blast creates 500 tons of rubble, 5% of which is lead.

277
00:15:17,521 --> 00:15:20,620
is lead.

278
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:23,120
Two hours later, a second crew moves in,

279
00:15:23,220 --> 00:15:26,860
with heavy machinery to haul it away.

280
00:15:26,960 --> 00:15:29,460
Lead in this form poses little threat to the miners,

281
00:15:29,560 --> 00:15:33,500
since it hasn't had a chance to react with the atmosphere.

282
00:15:33,600 --> 00:15:36,580
It's considered to have low bioavailability, which

283
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:40,740
means the body doesn't easily absorb it.

284
00:15:40,840 --> 00:15:42,880
But as a precaution, Doe Run miners

285
00:15:42,980 --> 00:15:47,520
are required to have a blood lead test once a year.

286
00:15:47,521 --> 00:15:51,520
After the ore is crushed, it ascends to the surface

287
00:15:51,620 --> 00:15:56,000
in buckets called skips, nine tons at a time.

288
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:58,740
Then it's up to the mill to get the lead out.

289
00:15:58,840 --> 00:16:01,040
The job here in the mill is to take the ores

290
00:16:01,140 --> 00:16:02,540
that comes from underground.

291
00:16:02,640 --> 00:16:05,300
It typically runs about 5% lead.

292
00:16:05,400 --> 00:16:07,900
We process that and produce a concentrate

293
00:16:08,000 --> 00:16:11,140
that has about 80% lead in it.

294
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:13,640
After undergoing further crushing here,

295
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:17,520
the ore fragments enter these rotating cylinders.

296
00:16:17,521 --> 00:16:21,520
Inside, they tumble with 80 tons of steel rods,

297
00:16:21,620 --> 00:16:24,720
three inches in diameter, which pulverize them.

298
00:16:24,820 --> 00:16:26,520
This is where we add water.

299
00:16:26,620 --> 00:16:29,460
So from this point on, the ore goes through the mill

300
00:16:29,560 --> 00:16:30,860
as a slurry.

301
00:16:30,960 --> 00:16:35,580
We also add the chemicals for the flotation process.

302
00:16:35,680 --> 00:16:38,680
This is a lead flotation circuit.

303
00:16:38,780 --> 00:16:41,180
In the flotation tank, both the chemical

304
00:16:41,280 --> 00:16:44,580
and physical process occur.

305
00:16:44,680 --> 00:16:46,820
Chemicals latch onto the lead minerals,

306
00:16:46,821 --> 00:16:49,620
minerals, and air bubbles introduced into the tank

307
00:16:49,720 --> 00:16:53,260
float the bonded particles to the surface.

308
00:16:53,360 --> 00:16:55,420
So what we see here is lead mineral

309
00:16:55,520 --> 00:16:59,360
floating over the sides of the flotation machine.

310
00:16:59,460 --> 00:17:00,360
That's right.

311
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:03,480
You're actually seeing this heavy metal float.

312
00:17:03,580 --> 00:17:05,040
At this point, the lead concentrate

313
00:17:05,140 --> 00:17:09,380
is diluted with about 80% water by weight.

314
00:17:09,480 --> 00:17:13,720
Large vacuum filters suck most of the water away,

315
00:17:13,721 --> 00:17:16,820
resulting in a concentrate 93% solid.

316
00:17:20,460 --> 00:17:23,900
On average, 60% of the concentrate produced here

317
00:17:23,901 --> 00:17:26,140
makes its way by covered truck to Herculaneum

318
00:17:26,141 --> 00:17:30,460
and the primary lead smelter, which the Doe Run Company

319
00:17:30,461 --> 00:17:32,040
has operated since 1986.

320
00:17:32,880 --> 00:17:35,360
The other 40% is sold worldwide.

321
00:17:35,560 --> 00:17:37,220
The primary purpose of the facility

322
00:17:37,221 --> 00:17:40,400
is to convert lead concentrate into lead metal.

323
00:17:41,360 --> 00:17:43,000
Loaders kick off the process

324
00:17:43,001 --> 00:17:45,300
by transferring each truckload of lead concentrate

325
00:17:45,400 --> 00:17:46,140
to a rail car.

326
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:51,540
Every 100-ton load enters the facility,

327
00:17:51,640 --> 00:17:54,340
where the first challenge is to remove whatever traces of sulfur

328
00:17:54,440 --> 00:17:57,440
remain.

329
00:17:57,540 --> 00:17:59,960
A furnace known as a sinter machine

330
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:03,320
burns off the sulfur by heating the concentrate to 1,500 degrees

331
00:18:03,420 --> 00:18:04,120
Fahrenheit.

332
00:18:07,020 --> 00:18:10,260
This converts the concentrate to lead oxide.

333
00:18:10,261 --> 00:18:12,660
In this form, it's more easily absorbed,

334
00:18:12,760 --> 00:18:14,560
so the workers here wear protective gear.

335
00:18:17,560 --> 00:18:20,000
The lead oxide moves to another furnace,

336
00:18:20,100 --> 00:18:22,480
where a mixture of carbon and oxygen

337
00:18:22,580 --> 00:18:27,080
generate a more intense heat of 2,500 degrees.

338
00:18:27,180 --> 00:18:28,740
This creates the proper conditions

339
00:18:28,840 --> 00:18:31,040
to convert the lead oxide to metallic lead.

340
00:18:33,880 --> 00:18:36,320
But smelting a metal that's also a neurotoxin

341
00:18:36,420 --> 00:18:40,260
produces emissions that carry inherent environmental risks.

342
00:18:40,261 --> 00:18:43,560
Although Doe Run has reduced its emissions here significantly

343
00:18:43,660 --> 00:18:46,660
over the past two decades, its smelting operation

344
00:18:46,760 --> 00:18:49,140
has often failed to meet state and federal environmental

345
00:18:49,240 --> 00:18:51,540
standards.

346
00:18:51,640 --> 00:18:54,200
Lead has contaminated the yards, homes, and children

347
00:18:54,300 --> 00:18:56,280
of Herculaneum's residents.

348
00:18:56,380 --> 00:18:58,240
And according to The New York Times,

349
00:18:58,340 --> 00:19:02,020
Doe Run has spent millions in cleanup efforts.

350
00:19:02,120 --> 00:19:04,960
The company continues to face criticism about its environmental

351
00:19:05,060 --> 00:19:07,520
track record.

352
00:19:07,521 --> 00:19:10,960
But our need for lead continues, and each year,

353
00:19:11,060 --> 00:19:14,700
150,000 tons or more of lead emerges from this facility.

354
00:19:17,960 --> 00:19:20,760
Not all of Earth's lead is laying around for just a few hundred

355
00:19:20,860 --> 00:19:23,040
million years like the ore mined here.

356
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:28,700
Much of it began its life over 4 billion years ago

357
00:19:28,800 --> 00:19:31,380
as a different, unstable element.

358
00:19:31,480 --> 00:19:34,640
LEAD IS THE END PRODUCT OF RADIOACTIVE DECAY.

359
00:19:34,641 --> 00:19:36,980
decay, uranium decays into lead.

360
00:19:37,080 --> 00:19:39,520
A lot of other radioactive elements decay into lead.

361
00:19:39,620 --> 00:19:43,120
And that's where a lot of our lead comes from.

362
00:19:43,220 --> 00:19:45,120
It's that decay which allows scientists

363
00:19:45,220 --> 00:19:48,820
like Dr. Roland Mundell to use lead as a key to unlock

364
00:19:48,920 --> 00:19:51,760
secrets of Earth's past.

365
00:19:51,860 --> 00:19:54,620
By determining the proportional amounts of uranium and lead

366
00:19:54,720 --> 00:19:58,000
within a rock sample, Dr. Mundell can calculate

367
00:19:58,100 --> 00:19:59,460
the sample's age.

368
00:19:59,560 --> 00:20:02,760
An hourglass is a good analogy of what is happening

369
00:20:02,761 --> 00:20:05,760
with the uranium lead system or any isotopic system.

370
00:20:05,761 --> 00:20:09,760
In this case, if we consider the upper part of the hourglass

371
00:20:09,761 --> 00:20:14,760
being uranium and the lower part the decay product, the lead,

372
00:20:14,761 --> 00:20:17,760
we can then precisely determine an age.

373
00:20:17,761 --> 00:20:20,760
So, for example, where we have a lot of uranium

374
00:20:20,761 --> 00:20:23,760
and very little lead accumulated,

375
00:20:23,761 --> 00:20:25,760
those would be relatively young ages.

376
00:20:25,761 --> 00:20:28,760
Where we have very little uranium left

377
00:20:28,761 --> 00:20:31,760
and a lot of lead accumulated,

378
00:20:31,761 --> 00:20:35,100
we would have very old ages.

379
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:37,300
This powder is a crushed volcanic rock

380
00:20:37,400 --> 00:20:40,000
sample from Australia.

381
00:20:40,100 --> 00:20:42,800
Determining its age will tell scientists precisely

382
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:45,340
when a mass extinction occurred during the Earth's Permian

383
00:20:45,440 --> 00:20:47,140
period.

384
00:20:47,240 --> 00:20:50,740
The first step in dating it is to isolate microscopic traces

385
00:20:50,840 --> 00:20:54,520
of lead and its unstable uranium parent.

386
00:20:54,620 --> 00:20:58,520
Magnets separate the granules that contain no lead.

387
00:20:58,521 --> 00:21:02,020
What we are interested in is the non-magnetic fraction

388
00:21:02,120 --> 00:21:06,760
right here, which contains tiny little amounts

389
00:21:06,860 --> 00:21:10,060
of zircon minerals.

390
00:21:10,160 --> 00:21:12,760
Next, the lead-bearing minerals enter a solution

391
00:21:12,860 --> 00:21:15,540
that separates them from lighter minerals.

392
00:21:15,640 --> 00:21:18,640
The lead-bearing ones collect at the bottom.

393
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:21,280
The most pristine of these isolated minerals, seen here

394
00:21:21,380 --> 00:21:25,920
under a microscope, will undergo a crucial treatment.

395
00:21:25,921 --> 00:21:30,160
The next step is adding a known amount of tracer solution,

396
00:21:30,260 --> 00:21:35,460
which contains artificial isotopes of uranium and lead.

397
00:21:35,560 --> 00:21:39,500
And in doing so, we can then later back calculate

398
00:21:39,600 --> 00:21:43,840
the ratio of uranium to lead in our sample.

399
00:21:46,400 --> 00:21:49,140
Later in the process, the microscopic traces of lead

400
00:21:49,240 --> 00:21:52,140
and uranium are loaded onto a turret, which then enters a

401
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:55,920
device called a mass spectrometer.

402
00:21:55,921 --> 00:21:58,820
The machine determines precisely how much uranium and lead

403
00:21:58,920 --> 00:22:01,920
exist within the sample.

404
00:22:02,020 --> 00:22:05,660
This enables Dr. Mundil to determine the sample's age.

405
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:08,360
For this volcanic rock from Australia,

406
00:22:08,460 --> 00:22:15,080
we calculate a uranium lead age of roughly 259 million years

407
00:22:15,180 --> 00:22:19,680
with an uncertainty of plus minus 400,000 years.

408
00:22:19,780 --> 00:22:21,480
Just as a few particles of lead

409
00:22:21,580 --> 00:22:25,380
can help us understand Earth's past,

410
00:22:25,381 --> 00:22:28,080
It takes little more to imperil our future.

411
00:22:34,520 --> 00:22:37,720
According to the Department of Housing and Urban Development,

412
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:39,460
one in every four American homes

413
00:22:39,560 --> 00:22:41,900
contains significant lead-based paint hazards.

414
00:22:44,560 --> 00:22:48,440
When the paint deteriorates, it frees chips and dust

415
00:22:48,540 --> 00:22:51,440
to contaminate everything in the area.

416
00:22:51,540 --> 00:22:53,500
Those most at risk are children.

417
00:22:53,501 --> 00:22:55,860
A child under the age of six exposed to lead

418
00:22:55,960 --> 00:22:59,100
absorbs it at twice the rate as an adult.

419
00:22:59,200 --> 00:23:00,900
And so it affects the nervous system

420
00:23:01,000 --> 00:23:03,440
and it may be detriment to their learning capabilities

421
00:23:03,540 --> 00:23:05,840
of the future.

422
00:23:05,940 --> 00:23:08,940
This lead of pigment team in Los Angeles, California,

423
00:23:09,040 --> 00:23:13,160
is among many nationwide addressing the problem.

424
00:23:13,260 --> 00:23:17,800
Its job is to remove the lead that until 1978 was added to paint

425
00:23:17,900 --> 00:23:23,500
to reduce corrosion and as a pigment to increase brightness.

426
00:23:23,501 --> 00:23:28,240
The team's target today is the house's exterior.

427
00:23:28,340 --> 00:23:31,900
The interior work will follow, but first a specialist must

428
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:36,040
determine how much lead the paint there contains.

429
00:23:36,140 --> 00:23:40,320
His X-ray fluorescence lead paint analyzer, or XRF,

430
00:23:40,420 --> 00:23:43,760
measures the milligrams of lead paint per square centimeter,

431
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:47,920
even if several lead-free paint layers are covering it.

432
00:23:48,020 --> 00:23:52,420
In LA County, an XRF reading higher than 0.7 is considered

433
00:23:52,421 --> 00:23:55,160
is considered a lead paint hazard.

434
00:23:55,260 --> 00:23:56,560
Here we have a ceiling.

435
00:23:56,660 --> 00:23:57,420
It's damaged.

436
00:23:57,520 --> 00:23:58,560
The paint is damaged.

437
00:23:58,660 --> 00:24:00,360
It's chipping, peeling.

438
00:24:00,460 --> 00:24:04,060
It has some mold damage.

439
00:24:04,160 --> 00:24:06,060
The reading is 9.6.

440
00:24:06,160 --> 00:24:08,660
That has a significant amount of lead in it.

441
00:24:08,760 --> 00:24:10,080
It should be treated.

442
00:24:10,180 --> 00:24:13,580
One of the major areas of concern for lead-based paint

443
00:24:13,680 --> 00:24:18,080
hazards are friction areas, such as window sashes rubbing up

444
00:24:18,180 --> 00:24:20,180
against window jams, window sills.

445
00:24:20,181 --> 00:24:23,520
that friction creates a fine dust that children

446
00:24:23,620 --> 00:24:25,280
can get their hands on.

447
00:24:25,380 --> 00:24:29,020
Hands can go in the mouth, and they ingest the lead that way.

448
00:24:29,120 --> 00:24:32,920
And this one is not in the greatest shape, and it's, yep,

449
00:24:33,020 --> 00:24:35,120
and has a significant amount of lead in it.

450
00:24:37,840 --> 00:24:40,340
Outside, the abatement team removes the lead paint

451
00:24:40,440 --> 00:24:44,040
on the house's exterior using only water and scrapers.

452
00:24:44,140 --> 00:24:46,740
We spray it down to keep the dust particles down.

453
00:24:46,840 --> 00:24:49,640
We're not allowed to do any mechanical sanding

454
00:24:49,641 --> 00:24:52,040
because of the amount of dust that gets created.

455
00:24:52,140 --> 00:24:54,640
So pretty much everything has to be manually scraped

456
00:24:54,740 --> 00:24:58,140
on a house like this.

457
00:24:58,240 --> 00:25:01,580
Once the scraping is complete, a vacuum

458
00:25:01,680 --> 00:25:06,120
sucks up the paint chips and dust for proper disposal.

459
00:25:06,220 --> 00:25:08,620
To contain the remaining lead paint that can't be scraped

460
00:25:08,720 --> 00:25:12,200
away, the crew sprays on a special coating called

461
00:25:12,300 --> 00:25:13,900
encapsulant.

462
00:25:14,000 --> 00:25:16,400
It bonds to the lead molecules.

463
00:25:16,401 --> 00:25:21,700
It has a 20-year lifespan, and it has something else called

464
00:25:21,800 --> 00:25:23,040
bitter mite in it.

465
00:25:23,140 --> 00:25:24,640
And it gives it a bitter taste.

466
00:25:24,740 --> 00:25:29,320
So if a child wants to fight or find a flake or have that bitter

467
00:25:29,420 --> 00:25:33,220
mite in it, it'll give them a sour aftertaste.

468
00:25:33,320 --> 00:25:36,680
The taste will probably stay with them for about two to three

469
00:25:36,780 --> 00:25:41,500
days, so they'll know not to eat that again.

470
00:25:41,600 --> 00:25:43,860
Lead-based paint removal from houses

471
00:25:43,861 --> 00:25:45,660
is an ongoing effort.

472
00:25:45,760 --> 00:25:48,360
But a significant amount of lead still remains in our soil.

473
00:25:50,960 --> 00:25:55,600
And it didn't all come from deteriorating lead paint.

474
00:25:55,700 --> 00:25:58,340
Most of it spewed from our cars as exhaust,

475
00:25:58,440 --> 00:26:02,140
starting in the 1920s when oil companies added it to gasoline

476
00:26:02,240 --> 00:26:05,380
to help prevent engine knock.

477
00:26:05,480 --> 00:26:07,520
Gasoline in an internal combustion engine

478
00:26:07,620 --> 00:26:10,220
is designed to ignite when the pistons complete their stroke

479
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:11,520
at the top of the cylinders.

480
00:26:13,860 --> 00:26:17,360
But gasoline in early engines often ignited prematurely,

481
00:26:17,361 --> 00:26:20,260
causing an audible knock or ping vibration

482
00:26:20,261 --> 00:26:22,860
and reduced efficiency.

483
00:26:22,861 --> 00:26:26,180
Adding a compound called tetraethyl lead to the gasoline

484
00:26:26,181 --> 00:26:28,940
solved the problem by helping the air fuel mixture

485
00:26:28,941 --> 00:26:31,720
to burn more evenly.

486
00:26:31,721 --> 00:26:34,180
But it also released harmful lead particulates

487
00:26:34,181 --> 00:26:36,220
in the engine's exhaust.

488
00:26:36,221 --> 00:26:39,860
50 years of lead was going into the atmosphere

489
00:26:39,861 --> 00:26:43,080
via the tailpipes of our cars.

490
00:26:43,081 --> 00:26:46,680
So there was leaded gasoline sitting there on the blacktop,

491
00:26:46,780 --> 00:26:50,860
eating it away, going, washing off in the next rainstorm

492
00:26:50,960 --> 00:26:52,360
into the water supply.

493
00:26:52,460 --> 00:26:53,860
Lead doesn't go away.

494
00:26:53,960 --> 00:26:56,400
It just stays there.

495
00:26:56,500 --> 00:26:59,560
Since the 70s, when lead was outlawed as a gasoline

496
00:26:59,660 --> 00:27:03,660
additive, lead levels in the blood of the American population

497
00:27:03,760 --> 00:27:06,300
have gone down 75%.

498
00:27:06,400 --> 00:27:08,300
Amazing.

499
00:27:08,400 --> 00:27:09,880
Over the years, we've learned there's

500
00:27:09,881 --> 00:27:12,780
not only in outlawing or disposing of lead,

501
00:27:12,880 --> 00:27:15,880
but also in recycling it.

502
00:27:15,980 --> 00:27:20,320
America recycles more than 1.2 million tons of lead per year

503
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:23,120
at sites like Doe Run's Buick Resource Recycling

504
00:27:23,220 --> 00:27:26,260
Facility in Missouri.

505
00:27:26,360 --> 00:27:29,240
Every day, 50 truckloads of lead-bearing materials

506
00:27:29,340 --> 00:27:32,140
arrive at the plant, from spent ammunition,

507
00:27:32,240 --> 00:27:36,240
to pipes and wheel weights, to cathode ray tubes.

508
00:27:36,340 --> 00:27:38,640
This is a CRT, or cathode ray tube,

509
00:27:38,641 --> 00:27:41,640
that might come from your computer or a TV screen.

510
00:27:41,740 --> 00:27:44,340
This is a 17-inch screen right here,

511
00:27:44,440 --> 00:27:47,280
and it'll contain about 25 pounds of silica,

512
00:27:47,380 --> 00:27:50,380
about 2.2 to 2.5 pounds of lead.

513
00:27:50,480 --> 00:27:53,780
During the year, the recycling plant is going to feed through

514
00:27:53,880 --> 00:27:58,100
about 8.9 million pounds of CRT material

515
00:27:58,200 --> 00:28:02,400
and recover about 500,000 pounds of lead.

516
00:28:02,500 --> 00:28:04,600
But this and the rest of the lead-bearing scrap

517
00:28:04,601 --> 00:28:09,940
accounts for only 15% of what's recycled here.

518
00:28:10,040 --> 00:28:15,580
You can probably guess what makes up the other 85%.

519
00:28:15,680 --> 00:28:20,220
The majority of our feeds are batteries, lead acid batteries.

520
00:28:23,080 --> 00:28:26,760
About 14 million of them this year.

521
00:28:26,860 --> 00:28:29,720
The 18 pounds of lead in the typical car battery

522
00:28:29,820 --> 00:28:32,420
must be separated from the sulfuric acid in the battery

523
00:28:32,421 --> 00:28:35,720
case.

524
00:28:35,820 --> 00:28:38,960
Inside this bunker, a loader dumps the spent batteries

525
00:28:39,060 --> 00:28:40,920
into a crusher.

526
00:28:41,020 --> 00:28:46,360
Its stainless steel teeth shred 30,000 batteries every day.

527
00:28:46,460 --> 00:28:49,400
But gallons upon gallons of acid collect onto the floor

528
00:28:49,500 --> 00:28:53,100
for later processing.

529
00:28:53,200 --> 00:28:54,600
At this point, a chemical process

530
00:28:54,700 --> 00:28:59,640
is used to remove a majority of the sulfur remaining in the lead.

531
00:28:59,740 --> 00:29:01,380
Chemicals added elsewhere in the facility

532
00:29:01,381 --> 00:29:02,880
will remove whatever traces linger.

533
00:29:05,580 --> 00:29:08,720
Then those chemicals must be burned off in a furnace.

534
00:29:08,820 --> 00:29:10,620
The molten lead exiting the furnace

535
00:29:10,720 --> 00:29:13,520
still contains some impurities.

536
00:29:13,620 --> 00:29:16,460
So workers in the refinery follow a detailed process

537
00:29:16,560 --> 00:29:20,600
to remove this so-called dross.

538
00:29:20,700 --> 00:29:23,740
We'll use chemicals such as sodium nitrate

539
00:29:23,840 --> 00:29:27,100
and sodium hydroxide to remove materials that were able to

540
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:28,880
oxidize out of the lead.

541
00:29:28,881 --> 00:29:30,920
Those materials will float to the top.

542
00:29:31,020 --> 00:29:33,760
Then we'll use our overhead crane and our operators

543
00:29:33,860 --> 00:29:37,060
to manually remove the dross from the surface of the lead.

544
00:29:37,160 --> 00:29:39,120
Once we've removed the dross, we basically

545
00:29:39,220 --> 00:29:41,660
have a pure lead alloy, which is ready then

546
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:43,500
for further processing.

547
00:29:43,600 --> 00:29:46,360
From the refinery, a propane-fueled trail of fire

548
00:29:46,460 --> 00:29:49,460
keeps the lead in liquid form and feeds it to the casting

549
00:29:49,560 --> 00:29:51,000
area.

550
00:29:51,100 --> 00:29:54,600
Within seconds, each ingot solidifies.

551
00:29:54,700 --> 00:29:57,240
This is an example of our 60-pound inline casting

552
00:29:57,340 --> 00:29:58,880
machine.

553
00:29:58,881 --> 00:30:01,520
This lead will be bundled into 2,000 pound bundles,

554
00:30:01,620 --> 00:30:03,120
and then it will be sent to our customer

555
00:30:03,220 --> 00:30:05,520
for final consumption.

556
00:30:05,620 --> 00:30:06,620
Who knows?

557
00:30:06,720 --> 00:30:09,720
With 1.2 million tons of lead recycled each year,

558
00:30:09,820 --> 00:30:11,520
the lead in your old computer monitor

559
00:30:11,620 --> 00:30:15,120
might end up starting your new car.

560
00:30:15,220 --> 00:30:18,640
But not all of our lead lives an industrial life.

561
00:30:18,740 --> 00:30:20,540
It has an artistic purpose as well.

562
00:30:25,140 --> 00:30:27,540
One of lead's most enduring applications

563
00:30:27,541 --> 00:30:31,780
is also its deadliest, the bullet.

564
00:30:31,880 --> 00:30:35,020
Most bullets made today have a hard copper or brass jacket

565
00:30:35,120 --> 00:30:37,720
covering a soft lead core.

566
00:30:37,820 --> 00:30:41,260
In bullets with hollow points, lead softness maximizes

567
00:30:41,360 --> 00:30:43,760
the bullet's stopping power.

568
00:30:43,860 --> 00:30:46,400
On impact, the metal jacket heels back,

569
00:30:46,500 --> 00:30:51,800
exposing the lead core, which mushrooms against the target.

570
00:30:51,900 --> 00:30:53,660
The earliest generations of bullets,

571
00:30:53,760 --> 00:30:56,540
spanning the 13th to 19th centuries,

572
00:30:56,541 --> 00:30:59,440
were simple lead balls.

573
00:30:59,540 --> 00:31:02,040
Lead's low melting point and its malleability

574
00:31:02,140 --> 00:31:04,440
enabled even the most isolated frontiersmen

575
00:31:04,540 --> 00:31:07,180
to easily cast them.

576
00:31:07,280 --> 00:31:09,080
The lead's melted now.

577
00:31:09,180 --> 00:31:10,780
It's a bright silver color.

578
00:31:10,880 --> 00:31:13,300
It's normally just kind of a dull gray.

579
00:31:13,400 --> 00:31:15,000
But when it melts, it turns silver.

580
00:31:15,100 --> 00:31:16,900
So we're ready to mold the bullet.

581
00:31:17,000 --> 00:31:20,100
So we'll ladle out some molten lead.

582
00:31:20,200 --> 00:31:23,440
And we'll pour it into the mold.

583
00:31:23,540 --> 00:31:25,540
Wait a little bit for it to cool.

584
00:31:25,541 --> 00:31:29,480
And then open it up.

585
00:31:29,580 --> 00:31:31,840
The only remaining step was to remove the ball

586
00:31:31,940 --> 00:31:36,680
from the mold and cut off the excess, called the sprue.

587
00:31:36,780 --> 00:31:38,780
After the sprues cut off, you end up

588
00:31:38,880 --> 00:31:45,700
with a .50 caliber round ball that can be fired from a rifle.

589
00:31:45,800 --> 00:31:48,300
Though easy to make, the round lead projectile

590
00:31:48,400 --> 00:31:49,300
had a key flaw.

591
00:31:52,040 --> 00:31:54,300
Its accuracy was severely limited,

592
00:31:54,301 --> 00:31:57,100
Because within the smoothbore muskets that fired it,

593
00:31:57,200 --> 00:32:00,880
the ball bounced from side to side in the barrel.

594
00:32:00,980 --> 00:32:03,940
19th century innovators improved lead projectiles

595
00:32:04,040 --> 00:32:08,520
by reshaping them into cone-shaped slugs,

596
00:32:08,620 --> 00:32:11,120
which traveled snugly through rifled gun barrels that

597
00:32:11,220 --> 00:32:14,180
gave the bullets a spiral twist.

598
00:32:14,280 --> 00:32:18,920
This made them go straighter, farther, and faster,

599
00:32:19,020 --> 00:32:21,620
paving the way for today's version of hot lead.

600
00:32:24,300 --> 00:32:28,900
Environmental concerns have driven a recent innovation,

601
00:32:29,000 --> 00:32:32,900
lead-free green bullets, the cores of tungsten, tin,

602
00:32:33,000 --> 00:32:33,800
or nylon.

603
00:32:37,640 --> 00:32:39,840
Surprisingly, the same utilitarian metal

604
00:32:39,940 --> 00:32:44,260
we use to make bullets also lends glass exceptional clarity.

605
00:32:47,660 --> 00:32:50,260
Lead crystal has been a sparkling dimension of lead

606
00:32:50,360 --> 00:32:51,660
since 1676.

607
00:32:54,300 --> 00:32:57,400
In order to be called lead crystal legally,

608
00:32:57,500 --> 00:33:00,780
it has to have a minimum of 24% lead.

609
00:33:00,880 --> 00:33:04,080
And a maximum in most manufacturing processes

610
00:33:04,180 --> 00:33:06,680
is 33%.

611
00:33:06,780 --> 00:33:08,540
With that much lead in the glass,

612
00:33:08,640 --> 00:33:11,020
how is it that the crystal becomes so sparklingly

613
00:33:11,120 --> 00:33:11,820
transparent?

614
00:33:14,060 --> 00:33:16,960
Lead crystal is a combination of lead oxide

615
00:33:17,060 --> 00:33:19,320
and the standard ingredients that comprise glass.

616
00:33:19,321 --> 00:33:24,320
When the mixture is heated to 2,200 degrees,

617
00:33:24,420 --> 00:33:28,320
a chemical reaction weakens the bonds between the lead molecules,

618
00:33:28,420 --> 00:33:31,960
negating much of their pigment.

619
00:33:32,060 --> 00:33:36,080
But the lead still retains one of its most distinctive properties,

620
00:33:36,160 --> 00:33:40,680
an ability to refract light much more than conventional glass.

621
00:33:40,780 --> 00:33:43,880
Blending lead crystal its increased brilliance.

622
00:33:43,980 --> 00:33:48,140
The addition of lead also increases the glass's density.

623
00:33:48,141 --> 00:33:50,900
But you won't notice the advantage this carries

624
00:33:51,000 --> 00:33:53,080
until it's time to make a toast.

625
00:33:53,180 --> 00:33:55,380
One of the benefits of adding lead to the glass,

626
00:33:55,480 --> 00:33:57,880
it gives it a distinctive tonal quality.

627
00:33:57,980 --> 00:33:59,880
So these are a beautiful pair of glasses,

628
00:33:59,980 --> 00:34:01,380
but these are glass.

629
00:34:01,480 --> 00:34:03,080
And they are machine-made.

630
00:34:03,180 --> 00:34:04,380
They're regular glass.

631
00:34:04,480 --> 00:34:10,000
And when I tap them together, it gives a nice ringing,

632
00:34:10,100 --> 00:34:11,600
but a little tinny.

633
00:34:11,700 --> 00:34:15,500
And now these are hand-blown leaded glass crystal.

634
00:34:15,501 --> 00:34:18,500
and listen to the quality of the tone in this one.

635
00:34:22,140 --> 00:34:26,140
It's much deeper and longer.

636
00:34:26,240 --> 00:34:29,240
Another of lead's properties, its pliability,

637
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:32,220
makes lead crystal a softer and more cooperative medium

638
00:34:32,320 --> 00:34:33,120
for engravers.

639
00:34:36,260 --> 00:34:38,220
As soon as I start to engrave this,

640
00:34:38,320 --> 00:34:41,100
I'll be able to feel how soft the lead is.

641
00:34:41,200 --> 00:34:43,620
I'll have much smoother cuts, and I'll

642
00:34:43,621 --> 00:34:45,160
And I'll be able to go much deeper

643
00:34:45,260 --> 00:34:48,760
than if it were just regular glass.

644
00:34:48,860 --> 00:34:52,560
The glass is much more brittle.

645
00:34:52,660 --> 00:34:55,260
You can hear the difference.

646
00:34:55,360 --> 00:34:59,940
And because of the brittleness of the glass,

647
00:35:00,040 --> 00:35:03,840
the drilling and engraving actually crack the glass.

648
00:35:03,940 --> 00:35:05,980
Now, if this were made out of leaded glass,

649
00:35:06,080 --> 00:35:08,580
this never would have happened.

650
00:35:08,680 --> 00:35:11,740
But lead crystal has one drawback.

651
00:35:11,741 --> 00:35:14,280
Over extended periods of time, the lead can leach

652
00:35:14,380 --> 00:35:18,040
into any liquid it contacts, especially acidic ones,

653
00:35:18,140 --> 00:35:18,940
like wine.

654
00:35:19,040 --> 00:35:22,980
Now, the amounts that are leached into your glass of wine

655
00:35:23,080 --> 00:35:26,080
when you pour it and you're consuming it during a meal

656
00:35:26,180 --> 00:35:27,680
are really pretty negligible.

657
00:35:27,780 --> 00:35:31,200
However, you definitely do not want to be storing liquids

658
00:35:31,300 --> 00:35:34,400
that you're going to consume later.

659
00:35:34,500 --> 00:35:37,440
Beyond that concern is the hefty price tag of a well-crafted

660
00:35:37,540 --> 00:35:40,240
piece of lead crystal.

661
00:35:40,241 --> 00:35:42,440
I'm not sure if that's an advantage or a drawback.

662
00:35:42,540 --> 00:35:43,640
It's an advantage for me.

663
00:35:46,940 --> 00:35:49,520
For more than 900 years, lead has

664
00:35:49,620 --> 00:35:51,860
played a role in another beautiful and delicate art

665
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:53,720
form, stained glass.

666
00:35:56,560 --> 00:35:59,560
Within each intricate panel, the many glass pieces

667
00:35:59,660 --> 00:36:04,200
are held in place by a network of lead strips called cane.

668
00:36:04,300 --> 00:36:07,740
Lead softness makes it the perfect form-fitting material,

669
00:36:07,741 --> 00:36:10,380
And its durability has helped some of history's most prized

670
00:36:10,480 --> 00:36:12,580
examples endure for centuries.

671
00:36:15,680 --> 00:36:18,780
The artists at Alpine's stained glass studio in San Diego,

672
00:36:18,880 --> 00:36:21,860
California value lead just as much as their old world

673
00:36:21,960 --> 00:36:23,560
predecessors.

674
00:36:23,660 --> 00:36:27,700
I don't know of any other metal that works as well for the

675
00:36:27,800 --> 00:36:29,620
purpose that we use it.

676
00:36:29,720 --> 00:36:31,400
We can form it.

677
00:36:31,500 --> 00:36:32,600
We can shape it.

678
00:36:32,700 --> 00:36:33,740
We can solder it.

679
00:36:33,840 --> 00:36:37,740
It's absolutely of ultimate value for a glassware.

680
00:36:37,741 --> 00:36:42,740
The artist starts each project by tracing a full-scale

681
00:36:42,840 --> 00:36:45,220
version of the design over the projected image

682
00:36:45,280 --> 00:36:48,420
of a smaller transparency.

683
00:36:48,480 --> 00:36:51,120
Among stained glass artists, these lines are known

684
00:36:51,180 --> 00:36:53,980
affectionately as the heart of the lead.

685
00:36:54,060 --> 00:36:57,260
The came begins as six-foot strips of lead, molded with

686
00:36:57,320 --> 00:37:01,360
grooves into which the glass pieces will fit.

687
00:37:01,440 --> 00:37:05,360
These grooves come in a variety of shapes and sizes.

688
00:37:05,361 --> 00:37:08,660
The artist starts with the panel's corner and begins

689
00:37:08,760 --> 00:37:10,300
assembling inward.

690
00:37:10,400 --> 00:37:13,100
Lead softness allows the artist to easily form the strips

691
00:37:13,200 --> 00:37:14,700
around each piece of glass.

692
00:37:16,900 --> 00:37:18,800
Solder secures the strips in place.

693
00:37:21,940 --> 00:37:23,740
And putty strengthens, weatherproofs,

694
00:37:23,840 --> 00:37:25,720
and polishes the final assembly.

695
00:37:29,320 --> 00:37:32,060
You might be wondering, do these pieces of leaded art

696
00:37:32,160 --> 00:37:33,720
pose any health hazards?

697
00:37:33,721 --> 00:37:36,560
I wouldn't suggest licking it or eating it,

698
00:37:36,660 --> 00:37:39,720
but as far as touching it or having it in your house,

699
00:37:39,820 --> 00:37:44,460
it's not going to be of any harm to anyone.

700
00:37:44,560 --> 00:37:47,960
Lead is an integral part of wonders not only to the eye,

701
00:37:48,060 --> 00:37:49,660
but also to the ear.

702
00:37:52,780 --> 00:37:54,500
Any guesses where the lead resides

703
00:37:54,600 --> 00:37:56,580
in the sweetest-sounding organs?

704
00:38:03,720 --> 00:38:15,560
For centuries, lead has unassumingly helped build the

705
00:38:15,660 --> 00:38:17,660
world with rich and resonant music.

706
00:38:24,780 --> 00:38:26,880
The organ in St. Joseph's Cathedral in Hartford,

707
00:38:26,980 --> 00:38:32,720
Connecticut has nearly 7,000 pipes, but only the largest are

708
00:38:32,721 --> 00:38:34,920
to churchgoers.

709
00:38:35,020 --> 00:38:37,820
Most of the smaller pipes are hidden from view,

710
00:38:37,920 --> 00:38:39,820
and all of those contain lead.

711
00:38:39,920 --> 00:38:43,060
There is no replacement for lead in an organ.

712
00:38:43,160 --> 00:38:44,860
You can't do it with aluminum.

713
00:38:44,960 --> 00:38:48,360
You can't cast them out of pottery.

714
00:38:48,460 --> 00:38:52,200
Organ pipes have always been and will continue

715
00:38:52,300 --> 00:38:56,180
to have their own unique quality and sound.

716
00:38:56,280 --> 00:38:58,280
Lead contributes to that unique sound

717
00:38:58,380 --> 00:39:00,720
with its inherent pliability.

718
00:39:00,721 --> 00:39:03,760
It allows the pipes to vibrate as the air pushes through,

719
00:39:03,860 --> 00:39:07,560
producing richer, fuller tones.

720
00:39:07,660 --> 00:39:09,760
In a huge organ like St. Joseph's,

721
00:39:09,860 --> 00:39:13,460
the longest pipes are typically made of zinc or tin.

722
00:39:13,560 --> 00:39:16,900
That's because zinc and tin are stronger and lighter than lead.

723
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:21,540
And lead pipes this long can't support their own weight.

724
00:39:21,640 --> 00:39:25,240
Even the shorter lead pipes need an added measure of strength.

725
00:39:25,340 --> 00:39:29,420
So they contain 50% to 70% tin.

726
00:39:29,421 --> 00:39:32,820
A mixture called spotted metal that doesn't compromise lead's

727
00:39:32,920 --> 00:39:35,720
tonal qualities.

728
00:39:35,820 --> 00:39:40,240
The organ at St. Joseph's was built here in 1960 at Austin

729
00:39:40,340 --> 00:39:43,600
Organ's in Hartford.

730
00:39:43,700 --> 00:39:46,540
Its craftsmen have been using lead to make beautiful music

731
00:39:46,640 --> 00:39:47,540
since 1893.

732
00:39:51,600 --> 00:39:55,940
In the company's furnace, lead and tin ingots melt at 750

733
00:39:55,941 --> 00:40:00,180
and mix together within minutes.

734
00:40:00,280 --> 00:40:03,540
Removing impurities is simply a matter of adding a little wax,

735
00:40:03,640 --> 00:40:06,880
which ignites a fiery process called flashing.

736
00:40:06,980 --> 00:40:08,680
The wax interacts with the metal

737
00:40:08,780 --> 00:40:11,480
and brings all the impurities that are left in the metal

738
00:40:11,580 --> 00:40:14,300
to the top.

739
00:40:14,400 --> 00:40:16,300
After skimming off the impurities,

740
00:40:16,400 --> 00:40:21,540
workers let the molten metal cool to about 450 degrees.

741
00:40:21,640 --> 00:40:23,540
Then they pour it into a trough

742
00:40:23,541 --> 00:40:28,740
and quickly run it to the end of a stone table covered with linen.

743
00:40:28,840 --> 00:40:31,940
The sheet solidifies within seconds.

744
00:40:32,040 --> 00:40:35,480
As the sheet cools, the lead separates from the tin,

745
00:40:35,580 --> 00:40:37,380
and spotted metal earns its name.

746
00:40:39,920 --> 00:40:41,920
After the sheets cure for two months

747
00:40:42,020 --> 00:40:45,160
to reduce their springiness, workers use a special plane

748
00:40:45,260 --> 00:40:46,460
to adjust their thickness.

749
00:40:46,461 --> 00:40:53,260
thickness. The thicker the pipe, the deeper

750
00:40:53,360 --> 00:40:57,300
its pitch. And the longer the pipe, the

751
00:40:57,400 --> 00:41:02,000
thicker the metal must be to support its weight.

752
00:41:02,100 --> 00:41:04,420
Workers complete the process by cutting the sheets to their

753
00:41:04,520 --> 00:41:09,280
prescribed lengths, rolling and beating them into shape, and

754
00:41:09,380 --> 00:41:13,860
joining the seam with solder. Then it's up to head voicer,

755
00:41:13,861 --> 00:41:17,760
Dan Kingman, to give each pipe its distinct voice.

756
00:41:17,860 --> 00:41:21,060
He does this by creating a slit, aptly called a mouth.

757
00:41:21,160 --> 00:41:24,100
The metal has to vibrate to a certain extent,

758
00:41:24,200 --> 00:41:28,400
sympathetically with the tone that's being generated

759
00:41:28,500 --> 00:41:30,100
by the wind blowing through the pipe.

760
00:41:30,200 --> 00:41:34,420
The tin lead composition has the advantage of being soft enough

761
00:41:34,520 --> 00:41:38,820
for the voicer to manipulate, to cut, to pare the metal away,

762
00:41:38,920 --> 00:41:43,860
to adjust, soft enough for that, but strong enough

763
00:41:43,861 --> 00:41:50,100
and responsive enough to make for good sounds.

764
00:41:52,200 --> 00:41:54,400
The slightest adjustment at the mouth of the pipe

765
00:41:54,500 --> 00:41:58,200
affects its sound, because it is here that compressed air,

766
00:41:58,300 --> 00:42:00,340
the organ's breath, exits.

767
00:42:02,540 --> 00:42:04,920
By widening a hole at the foot of the pipe,

768
00:42:05,020 --> 00:42:07,980
Dan adjusts the speech of the pipe further

769
00:42:08,080 --> 00:42:09,560
and also increases its volume.

770
00:42:09,561 --> 00:42:11,360
It's now speaking right.

771
00:42:11,460 --> 00:42:13,560
It's a little soft.

772
00:42:16,900 --> 00:42:18,560
So we'll make it a little bit louder.

773
00:42:24,200 --> 00:42:25,580
A permanent set of tuned pipes

774
00:42:25,680 --> 00:42:27,640
on the back row of the voicing machine

775
00:42:27,740 --> 00:42:29,180
provides a reference pitch.

776
00:42:31,820 --> 00:42:35,120
The pipe is flat.

777
00:42:35,220 --> 00:42:36,860
He finishes his voicing work

778
00:42:36,861 --> 00:42:40,100
by trimming the pipe's length and adjusting its so-called

779
00:42:40,200 --> 00:42:41,400
tuning collar.

780
00:42:41,500 --> 00:42:45,660
I activate that pipe and listen to it with the one in the front

781
00:42:45,760 --> 00:42:48,900
and listen for beats to see if they're in or out of tune.

782
00:42:58,920 --> 00:43:00,320
And that is roughly voiced.

